<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=egyptian-hieroglyphs">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Writing and counting</p><h1 class="enc-title">Egyptian hieroglyphs</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as hieroglyphics</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Egyptian hieroglyphs were the formal writing system of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, using pictorial signs for sounds and words. The peace treaty between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire</a> and Egypt after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kadesh">Kadesh</a> survives both in Akkadian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hattusa">Hattusa</a> and in hieroglyphs at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a>, illustrating the diplomatic ties between the two powers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/egyptian-hieroglyphs.jpg" alt="Egyptian hieroglyphs" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anonymous · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Minnakht_01.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Hieroglyphs combined ideographic, logographic, syllabic and alphabetic elements, with more than 1,000 distinct characters. They derive from proto-literate symbol systems of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-bronze-age">Early Bronze Age</a>, around the 33rd century BCE, such as the clay labels from Abydos attributed to a Predynastic ruler called Scorpion I. The first full sentence in mature hieroglyphs found so far is on a seal impression in the tomb of Seth-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peribsen">Peribsen</a>, of the Second Dynasty. About 900 signs were in use in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-francia">Middle Kingdom</a>, and by the Greco-Roman period there were more than 5,000.</p><p>Scholars have long debated whether the script arose independently or under the influence of Mesopotamian cuneiform. Geoffrey Sampson argued that Egyptian writing was probably invented under Sumerian influence, but the glyphs found at Abydos, dated between 3400 and 3200 BCE, have cast doubt on the older view that Mesopotamian writing came first.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The script has three kinds of sign: phonetic glyphs, including twenty-four single-consonant signs that work like an alphabet; logographs, which represent morphemes; and determinatives, silent signs at the end of a word that narrow its meaning. The writing normally does not indicate vowels, so some have called it an abjad. The same sign can be read in different ways according to context. Lines or columns are ordinarily read from right to left, and the direction in which animal and human figures face shows the reading order.</p><p>Cursive forms were used for religious literature on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a> and wood, and simplified forms gave rise to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hieratic">hieratic</a> and demotic scripts. Hieroglyphs nevertheless continued beside them in monumental and formal writing. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/proto-sinaitic-script">Proto-Sinaitic script</a> drew on them, and through it the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenician-alphabet">Phoenician alphabet</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Hieroglyphs continued to be used under Persian rule and in the Ptolemaic and Roman periods. Monumental use ceased after Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-i">Theodosius I</a> closed all non-Christian temples in 391, and the last known inscription, the Graffito of Esmet-Akhom at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philae">Philae</a>, dates from 394. Knowledge of the script was then lost completely. Medieval and early modern attempts failed because they assumed that the signs recorded ideas rather than sounds, and Athanasius Kircher in the 17th century was held back by belief in the mystical nature of the symbols.</p><p>The breakthrough came with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rosetta-stone">Rosetta Stone</a>, found by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s troops in 1799, which gives the same text in hieroglyphs, demotic and Greek. Silvestre de Sacy, Johan David Åkerblad and Thomas Young made some progress, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-francois-champollion">Jean-François Champollion</a> completed the decipherment by the 1820s.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Egyptian hieroglyphs” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its scribes used <b>Egyptian hieroglyphs</b> and Babylonian cuneiform, and an undeciphered local system known as the Byblos syllabic script appears on stone and bronze inscriptions of the second millennium BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byblos">Byblos · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Treaty of Kadesh, concluded between Hattusili III and Ramesses II around 1259 BCE, is the earliest peace treaty to survive in the versions of both parties: in Akkadian cuneiform at Hattusa and in <b>Egyptian hieroglyphs</b> at Karnak and the Ramesseum.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In Nubia, conquered by the New Kingdom pharaohs, Egyptian became the language of temples and government, and the kings of Kush who ruled Egypt in the 8th and 7th centuries BCE wrote in <b>Egyptian hieroglyphs</b>; the later Meroitic script of Kush was derived from Egyptian signs.</blockquote><a href="/language/ancient-egyptian">Ancient Egyptian · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>It is the last known ancient Egyptian inscription in <b>Egyptian hieroglyphs</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-24">24 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>c. 3250 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Abydos, Egypt</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Whether hieroglyphs arose independently or from Mesopotamian writing is debated; signs from Abydos as early as 3400 BCE question Mesopotamian priority.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Led to</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/proto-sinaitic-script">The first alphabet<span>c. 1850 BCE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=egyptian-hieroglyphs">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
